demux: an audio tap at the demuxer, for skip detection

Kaeri fingerprints the audio around openings and endings. The selected audio stream's
compressed packets are copied as they enter the demuxer cache, so at readahead speed and
ahead of playback, decoded on a thread of the tap's own and handed to the client as 16-bit
mono samples at 11025 Hz with their playback-timeline timestamps. A call with no samples is
a break: a seek, dropped packets, a track switch, the end of a window or of the file.

The client sets its callback with mpv_set_audio_tap(), one per mpv core and never through
an option, so a script or IPC client cannot point mpv at code. The options say what to tap:
demuxer-audio-tap-windows (up to four start-end ranges in seconds, empty and off by
default, read again for each packet so a runtime change applies to the packets after it),
demuxer-audio-tap-tag (handed back with every call, read when the demuxer opens) and
demuxer-audio-tap-max-bytes (the queue, 8 MiB by default, allocated once when the tap
starts). The demuxer only copies a packet into that queue under its lock; when the queue is
full the packet is dropped and the gap becomes a break, so the demuxer never waits. Audio
that was delivered once is not delivered again.

New files plus small hooks in demux.c, demux.h, player/client.c, player/client.h, the
public client.h and meson.build. For mpv v0.41.0: android/scripts/mpv.sh and
desktop/linux/scripts/libmpv.sh apply it to the release archive, apple/scripts/mpv.sh after
its own patches. LGPL-2.1-or-later, as mpv.

